ed": 0, "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "question", "contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "engenn9le12ob02re7pdf01.pdf"}]}]}], "template_override": 1, "id": 11, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " \nTalk about a trick you played on someone.\n"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Take Away"}]}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}]}}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}]}, {"type": "post_instructions", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "\nInstructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self Assessment.\n"}]}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}]}], "id": 0, "stimuli": [{"type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"coverimg": "engenn9le12ob02re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "engenn9le12ob02re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "engenn9le12ob02re2img01.jpg", "text": "", "id": 0}, {"time": "100", "image": "engenn9le12ob02re2img01.jpg", "text": "George has had a simple life. He has always dressed in black pants, a white shirt, and a gray cardigan. He has always eaten a soft-boiled egg and two pieces of toast for breakfast.<br/><br/>He goes to the park on Mondays and Wednesdays and to the library on Tuesdays and Thursdays. On Fridays he always visits his mother. On weekends he volunteers.<br/><br/>George hasn't had an easy life. It must have been difficult growing up blind. However, George hasn't complained. He's managed quite well in fact.", "id": 1}, {"time": "30000", "image": "engenn9le12ob02re2img02.jpg", "text": "A few years ago, some local troublemakers decided George was an easy target. They may have decided that because he followed such a tight schedule. They must have thought that tripping him and taking his keys was a good plan. <br/><br/>They must have assumed that with the keys two of them could burgle his house. They must have thought they could keep him occupied. <br/><br/>Monday came. The boys waited. Oddly, George never showed up. For some unfathomable reason, he visited his mother that day!", "id": 2}, {"time": "61000", "image": "engenn9le12ob02re2img03.jpg", "text": "George doesn't know what was supposed to happen that day. Only those boys who tried to rob him know the truth. Somehow it changed them. It may have showed them that it pays to be a good person. Now, all three are good citizens. <br/><br/>I have a degree in social work, Danny is in medical school, and Rob is a teacher. We still see George sometimes, feeding the pigeons, in the park.", "id": 3}]}}], "template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "id": 1, "_data": []}]}], "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Anecdotes and Personal Stories"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "George"}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 12}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.1"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe situations in written and spoken interactions according to social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn9le12ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Speculating About the Past"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use modals to talk about degrees of certainty and speculate about things in the past.<br/>You use them to describe what is definite, probable, possible, or impossible from the speaker's point of view. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use <span class=\"italic\">must</span>, <span class=\"italic\">may</span>, <span class=\"italic\">might</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">could</span> to describe degrees of certainty.<br/>You can use modals with the Present Perfect to talk about the possibility of a past event that is not clear in the present context."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">They <span class=\"strong\">must have thought</span> they could keep him occupied.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look at the chart for the meanings of modals when speculating about the past:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Modal Phrase\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Meaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Must have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" It is very likely\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"May have <br/>Might have<br/>Could have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" It is possible \\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li><span class=\"italic\">May have</span> has a slightly stronger meaning than <span class=\"italic\">might have</span> or <span class=\"italic\">could have</span>.</li><li>You can add <span class=\"italic\">not</span> to make a negative speculation (It <span class=\"italic\">may not have been</span> true).</li></ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "They may have decided that because he followed such a tight schedule. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "They must have thought that tripping him and taking his keys was a good plan. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "They must have assumed that with the keys two of them could burgle his house. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "They must have thought they could keep him occupied. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "It may have showed them that it pays to be a good person."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Present Perfect Tense vs. The Simple Past Tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Present Perfect tense to talk about a past action or an action that began in the past that is connected to the present in some way. <br/><br/>You use the Simple Past tense to talk about many kinds of past events: short, quickly finished actions, longer situations and repeated events. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use the <span class=\"underline\">Present Perfect tense</span> to talk about experience.<br/>When you use the Present Perfect tense, you think about the past and present together. There is some connection between what happened in the past and now.<br/><br/>For example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">I have learned English.</span> = I learned English in the past and now I can speak English.<br/><span class=\"italic\">He has bought a painting. </span>= He bought the painting in the past and now he has a painting.<br/><br/>It is common to use <span class=\"italic\">for</span> and <span class=\"italic\">since</span> when referring to time periods in the Present Perfect:<br/><br/><ul><li>You use FOR to talk about how long an action has been happening (a week, 2 days, five hours, six years etc.)</li><li>You use SINCE to talk about when the action started (2010, Monday, she was a child)</li></ul><br/><br/>Usually, when you use the <span class=\"underline\">Simple Past tense</span>, the time of the finished action is clear from the context or explicitly mentioned in the sentence.<br/><br/>For example:<br/><br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Yesterday</span>, I <span class=\"strong\">went</span> to the store. <br/><span class=\"strong\">A few years ago</span>, some local troublemakers <span class=\"strong\">decided</span> George was an easy target.</span><br/><br/>It is clear when the action occurred. In this sentence we would not use the Present Perfect. (<span style=\"text-decoration:line-through;\">Yesterday, I have been to the store)."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Monday came. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "The boys waited. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Oddly, George never showed up. For some unfathomable reason, he visited his mother that day!"}, {"type": "example", "content": "George has had a simple life. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "He has always dressed in black pants, a white shirt, and a gray cardigan. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "He has always eaten a soft-boiled egg and two pieces of toast for breakfast."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "cardigan"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "A sweater which fastens at the front with buttons"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He has always dressed in black pants, a white shirt, and a gray cardigan"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use to talk about troublemakers."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "soft-boiled "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "used to describe an egg which has been boiled with its shell on, and which has been removed from the water while the yellow part is still soft"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He has always eaten a soft-boiled egg and two pieces of toast for breakfast."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "troublemaker "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a person who intentionally causes problems for other people"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some local troublemakers decided George was an easy target."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "target "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the intended recipient of an attack"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: George was an easy target."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to trip "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "when a person loses his or her balance while walking or running"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They must have thought that tripping him and taking his keys was a good plan."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to assume "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to accept something as truth without question or proof"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They must have assumed that with the keys two of them could burgle his house."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "unfathomable "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "impossible to understand"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For some unfathomable reason, he visited his mother that day!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le12ob02aud07"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Think about a trick you played on someone and map out the events on a time line."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Note the person's feeling and reactions on the timeline."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Note where you talk about has happened or happened."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Use the timeline to recount the story aloud."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Practice it several times so it becomes natural."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Tell the story to a partner without your notes."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I map out events for a trick I placed on someone on a timeline."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I add vocabulary for feelings and reactions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use the Past Simple and Present Perfect tenses."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I retell the story aloud without notes."}]}]}